## Quick Answer

A main floor remodel usually combines three jobs: **a new kitchen, one or more walls opened between kitchen, dining and living rooms, and new flooring across the level**. The kitchen sets the budget. In Seattle a midrange major kitchen remodel averaged **$95,542** in the 2025 Cost vs Value report, and an upscale one **$188,401**. Removing a load-bearing wall adds roughly **$1,200 to $10,000** in national cost guides, and new floors run about **$4 to $25 per sq ft** installed depending on the material.

Removing a load-bearing wall needs a permit in Seattle, Bellevue, Kirkland and Redmond, and Kirkland requires one for any wall. In Bellevue the median review for a remodel permit with plan review was **13 days** over the past year.

## What a Main Floor Remodel Usually Includes

In many older ramblers and split-levels around Seattle and the Eastside, the main floor is a closed kitchen, a separate dining room and a living room with a wall or two between them. A main floor remodel turns that into one connected space. Typical scope:

- **Kitchen:** new layout, cabinets, counters, appliances, lighting, often an island where a wall used to be
- **Walls:** removing a non-bearing partition, or replacing a load-bearing wall with a beam
- **Floors:** one continuous floor through kitchen, dining and living areas
- **Systems:** moving plumbing, adding circuits for the island and lighting, rerouting ducts that ran through the old wall
- **Finishes:** drywall, trim, paint, doors

About 45% of homes in King County were built before 1980, according to the 2024 King County Comprehensive Plan. Age matters for two items below: asbestos and lead paint.

## What It Costs: The Components

### Kitchen (Seattle, 2025 Cost vs Value)

The [2025 Cost vs Value report](https://www.jlconline.com/cost-vs-value/2025/pacific/seattle-wa/) prices standard projects for the Seattle market:

| Project | Seattle job cost | Value at resale | Recouped |
|---|---|---|---|
| Minor kitchen remodel, midrange (same layout) | $32,271 | $38,098 | 118.1% |
| Major kitchen remodel, midrange | $95,542 | $50,762 | 53.1% |
| Major kitchen remodel, upscale | $188,401 | $72,971 | 38.7% |

A main floor remodel almost always means a major kitchen remodel, because the layout changes when the wall comes out.

### Removing a Wall (National Cost Guides)

| Wall type | Typical range |
|---|---|
| Non-load-bearing wall | $300 to $2,000 |
| Load-bearing wall, one-story house | $1,200 to $10,000 |
| Load-bearing wall, multi-story house | $3,000 to $15,000 |
| Structural engineer | $200 to $1,400 |
| Steel beam | $1,000 to $5,000 |

Ranges combine [Angi](https://www.angi.com/articles/how-much-does-it-cost-remove-load-bearing-wall.htm), [HomeGuide](https://homeguide.com/costs/cost-to-remove-a-wall) and [HomeAdvisor](https://www.homeadvisor.com/cost/architects-and-engineers/wall-removal-cost) 2025-2026 data, which are national averages. Seattle-area labor typically runs above national averages. The wall itself is rarely the expensive part: what is inside it is. Ducts, a plumbing vent or a gas line in the wall must be rerouted, and the floor and ceiling where the wall stood need patching or replacement.

### Flooring

National 2025 cost guides put **luxury vinyl plank at about $4 to $16 per sq ft installed** and **hardwood at about $6 to $25 per sq ft**, before tear-out and subfloor repair. Opening a wall leaves a strip where two old floors met, so plan on one new floor through the whole space rather than patching.

### Putting It Together

There is no single price for a main floor remodel because the kitchen dominates. A realistic way to budget is: the kitchen tier you want from the table above, plus the wall work, plus flooring for the full open area, plus 10-15% contingency for what you find inside the walls. For example, a midrange major kitchen ($95,542) with one load-bearing wall on a one-story house and 600 sq ft of LVP lands in roughly the $100,000-$120,000 range before contingency. Get a written estimate that lists each part separately.

## Permits: City by City

| City | When a permit is needed | Notes |
|---|---|---|
| Seattle | Any removal of load-bearing support, and alterations over $6,000 in six months | Most interior remodels go through a Subject-to-Field-Inspection (STFI) permit |
| Bellevue | Moving or removing a load-bearing wall, adding a beam or header | Remodel permit with plan review; median 13 days |
| Kirkland | Adding, removing or relocating any wall, load-bearing or not; interior remodels; drywall removal | Painting, flooring, cabinets and counters do not need a permit |
| Redmond | Alterations and remodels of an existing home | Painting, tiling, carpet, cabinets and counters do not need a permit |

### Seattle

Under the [Seattle Residential Code](https://www.seattle.gov/documents/Departments/SDCI/Codes/SeattleResidentialCode/2021SRCChapter1.pdf), minor alterations of $6,000 or less in any six months are exempt, but the exemption never covers removing load-bearing support. Installing cabinets, surface finishes, paint and insulation are exempt.

Most interior remodels of a house use an [STFI permit](https://www.seattle.gov/sdci/permits/permits-we-issue-(a-z)/construction-permit---subject-to-field-inspection), whose screening takes a few days. Per [SDCI Tip 316](https://www.seattle.gov/dpd/publications/cam/cam316.pdf), a new beam or header can go in without an engineer's stamp if it spans up to 8 ft with up to two stories and a roof above, or up to 14 ft with one story and a roof above. Longer spans, more stories above, steel beams beyond three, or flitch beams need stamped plans. Replacing walls in more than 60% of the house is likely a substantial alteration that triggers code upgrades for the whole house.

### Bellevue

Bellevue's [single-family remodel permit](https://bellevuewa.gov/city-government/departments/development/permits/residential-building-permits/single-family-remodel-permit) with plan review covers moving or removing a load-bearing wall, replacing it with a beam or adding a header, and includes the electrical, mechanical and plumbing permits. A kitchen remodel or removing a non-bearing wall can use the permit without plan review, usually issued the same day. Over the 12 months to October 2026, remodel permits with plan review took a median of **13 days** and an average of 35 days ([Bellevue permit processing data](https://bellevuewaprod.blob.core.windows.net/documentcenter/DSRecords/processing-day-by-permit-type.pdf)). Moving a fixture with new piping, or adding electrical circuits, needs a permit.

### Kirkland and Redmond

[Kirkland](https://www.kirklandwa.gov/Government/Departments/Development-Services-Center/Do-you-need-a-permit) requires a permit to add, remove or relocate any wall, load-bearing or not, for interior remodels and even for drywall removal. [Redmond](https://www.redmond.gov/469/Residential-Permits) requires one for alterations and remodels of an existing home. In both cities painting, tiling, carpet, cabinets and countertops alone do not.

## Older Homes: Asbestos and Lead

- **Asbestos.** Before renovation in the Puget Sound region, an asbestos survey is required. The [Puget Sound Clean Air Agency](https://pscleanair.gov/192/Homeowner-Renovation) lets an owner who lives in the house do their own survey for a remodel; in other cases an AHERA-certified inspector does it. Washington law ([RCW 49.26](https://app.leg.wa.gov/rcw/default.aspx?cite=49.26&full=true)) does not let a contractor start work without a copy of the written survey report. Drywall joint compound, popcorn ceilings, old vinyl flooring and duct wrap are the usual suspects in pre-1980 homes.
- **Lead paint.** In homes built before 1978, anyone paid to disturb painted surfaces must follow the EPA's [Renovation, Repair and Painting rule](https://www.epa.gov/lead/renovation-repair-and-painting-program); Washington runs the program and requires both renovator and firm certification.

## Energy Code When Walls Are Open

Washington's residential energy code ([WAC 51-11R-50300](https://app.leg.wa.gov/wac/default.aspx?cite=51-11R-50300)) requires that wall, ceiling and floor cavities opened during a remodel are filled with insulation: at least R-15 in 2x4 walls and R-21 in 2x6 walls. Replacement windows must meet the code's U-factor. When a remodel replaces more than 10% of light fixtures, all lamps must be high efficacy. Seattle applies its own energy code.

## Timeline

Planning takes longer than building. In the [2026 Houzz & Home study](https://st.hzcdn.com/static/econ/2026_U.S._Houzz_and_Home_Study_RenovationTrends.pdf) of 2025 projects, homeowners spent a median of 9.5 months planning a kitchen remodel and 5.8 months building it. For a main floor remodel around Seattle, plan for:

1. Design, engineering if a load-bearing wall is involved, and asbestos survey: 3-8 weeks
2. Permit: from a few days (Seattle STFI, Bellevue without plan review) to several weeks with plan review
3. Construction: typically 2-4 months for kitchen, wall and floors together, longer if cabinets or windows have long lead times

## Frequently Asked Questions

### How much does it cost to open up a kitchen to the living room?

The wall work alone is about $300-$2,000 for a non-bearing wall and roughly $1,200-$10,000 for a load-bearing wall in national guides, before patching floors and ceilings. Most homeowners redo the kitchen at the same time, which is the larger cost: $95,542 for a midrange major kitchen remodel in Seattle in 2025.

### Do I need a permit to remove a wall in Seattle?

Yes if the wall is load-bearing, whatever the cost. A non-bearing wall can be exempt only if total work stays at or under $6,000 in six months. Most projects use an STFI permit.

### How do I know if a wall is load-bearing?

Walls that run perpendicular to floor or ceiling joists and sit under a wall, beam or point load above are often load-bearing, but only an inspection of the framing confirms it. Exterior walls almost always carry load.

### Does a main floor remodel add value?

In the 2025 Cost vs Value data for Seattle, a same-layout kitchen refresh recouped 118% of its cost at resale, while a midrange major kitchen remodel recouped about 53%. The main return of an open main floor is how the house lives day to day.

